[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"JavaScript Functions: Declaration, Expression, and Arrow Functions"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Functions are fundamental building blocks in JavaScript. They allow you to encapsulate a block of code that performs a specific task. This tutorial explores three ways to define functions: function declarations, function expressions, and arrow functions."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Function Declarations"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Function declarations are the most common way to define functions. They are hoisted, meaning you can call the function before it appears in the code."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Function Declaration
function greet(name) {
  return 'Hello, ' + name + '!';
}

console.log(greet('World')); // Output: Hello, World!"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Syntax: `function functionName(parameters) { // function body }`"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Hoisted: Can be called before the declaration in the code."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Function Expressions"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Function expressions involve assigning a function to a variable. Unlike function declarations, function expressions are not hoisted."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"// Function Expression
const square = function(number) {
  return number * number;
};

console.log(square(5)); // Output: 25"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Syntax: `const variableName = function(parameters) { // function body };`"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Not Hoisted: Must be defined before being called."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Arrow Functions"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Arrow functions provide a more concise syntax for writing function expressions. They are particularly useful for short, simple functions."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// Arrow Function
const multiply = (a, b) => a * b;

console.log(multiply(3, 4)); // Output: 12"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Syntax: `(parameters) => expression` or `(parameters) => { // function body }`"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implicit Return: If the function body is a single expression, the `return` keyword can be omitted."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Not Hoisted: Must be defined before being called."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lexical `this` binding: Arrow functions do not have their own `this` value; they inherit it from the surrounding scope."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Choosing the Right Function Type"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"The choice between function declarations, function expressions, and arrow functions depends on the specific situation and coding style. Function declarations are useful when you need hoisting. Function expressions provide more flexibility in assigning functions to variables. Arrow functions offer a concise syntax, especially for simple functions, and are often preferred in modern JavaScript development."},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider the context and readability when selecting the appropriate function type for your code."}]
